content.text:
The user wants: "In the main dashboard when I pressed chard put 4 charts with different time frame 1min, 5min, 15min, 4hr four winds in the same page"

Interpretation: On the main dashboard, when they press "chart" (maybe a button/tab), show 4 charts with different timeframes (1m, 5m, 15m, 4h) — "four winds" likely means "four windows" (four chart panels) on the same page.

So replace/augment the single TradingView chart with a grid of 4 charts: 1m, 5m, 15m, 4h, all for the selected pair, displayed simultaneously in a 2x2 grid. The existing interval buttons could become a toggle between "single" and "quad" view? The user says "when I pressed chart put 4 charts ... four windows in the same page". So clicking the chart/View chart button should show the 4-chart grid. Simplest: make the main chart panel always a 2x2 grid of four TradingView widgets for 1m, 5m, 15m, 4h. Remove the interval buttons or keep them to highlight? Better:

- Replace the single chart with a "Multi-timeframe chart" section containing 4 chart cards (1m, 5m, 15m, 4h), each with its own TradingView widget.
- Keep the "View chart" button scrolling to it.
- Remove interval selector (or repurpose it as "single view" toggle). The user specifically wants 4 windows; implement 4 windows directly.
- Add a "Quad view" label and perhaps a button "Single" to switch back? Not required. But the user says "when I pressed chart put 4 charts" — maybe they want the 4-chart view triggered by the View chart button. However, a dashboard that always shows 4 charts is simpler and matches "in the same page".

Design:
```html
<section class="panel chart-panel">
  <div class="chart-head">
    <div class="chart-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Live charts · TradingView</p>
      <div class="chart-symbol-row">
        <h2 id="chart-symbol">BTCUSDT</h2>
        <span class="chart-price" id="chart-price">—</span>
        <span class="chart-change" id="chart-change">—</span>
      </div>
      <p class="chart-sub">Four timeframes, one pair — 1m trigger, 5m signal, 15m structure, 4h context.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-controls">
      <span class="chart-view-badge">4 windows</span>
      <a class="chart-link" ...>Open ↗</a>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="tv-grid" id="tv-grid">
    <div class="tv-cell" data-tf="1">
      <div class="tv-cell-head"><strong>1m</strong><span>Trigger</span></div>
      <div class="tv-slot" id="tv-1"></div>
    </div>
    ... 5m Signal, 15m Structure, 4h Context
  </div>
  <p class="chart-foot">Charts by TradingView · live Binance spot · click any signal or pair to load all four windows</p>
</section>
```

Remove the interval buttons and `chart-intervals` listener. Or keep a compact timeframe legend.

Implementation: `renderChart()` builds four widgets into `#tv-1`, `#tv-5`, `#tv-15`, `#tv-240`. Each script needs its own container. TradingView embed script creates an iframe inside its parent. We must append one script per slot.

Potential issue: multiple TradingView widgets on one page — fine, though heavier. 4 iframes may be slow but acceptable. Set chart height 240px each; grid 2x2 → total ~520px.

Fallback: if a widget fails, draw fallback sparkline for that timeframe using klines? We have only 5m klines; can approximate per timeframe by resampling or just show link. Simpler: each slot gets a loading placeholder; after 9s, if no iframe, show link text. Could fetch klines per timeframe for fallback, but extra. For the 4h chart fallback, use 5m closes resampled? Not necessary. Keep link fallback.

But the user might be in an environment where TradingView is blocked. Then all 4 fail. The previous single chart had a fallback sparkline. We can keep a fallback: fetch klines for each interval and draw sparkline. That's 4 extra requests only if widget fails. Implement `drawFallbackChart(slot, interval)` that fetches klines? Async. Simpler: use state.chartKlines (5m) for 5m slot; for other slots, fetch on demand. But this adds complexity. Alternatively show a message with link.

Given the user explicitly wants 4 charts, implement widgets + graceful fallback message.

Also "when I pressed chart" — the "View chart" button in trade ticket scrolls to the chart. That still works. Maybe also add a "Charts" button in header? The refresh button is "Scan now". Could add a "Charts" nav button that scrolls to the 4-chart grid. The ticket's "View chart" does that. Add a header button "Charts" with a grid icon? Nice. But keep scope.

Let me restructure the chart panel HTML and CSS, and rewrite renderChart.

Important: The user wrote "4hr" — include 4h. Timeframes: 1m, 5m, 15m, 4h. TradingView intervals: "1", "5", "15", "240".

Chart cell labels:
- 1m — Trigger
- 5m — Signal
- 15m — Structure
- 4h — Context

Each cell header shows timeframe + role + maybe live price? Not necessary.

CSS:
```css
.tv-grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0,1fr)); gap:10px; }
.tv-cell { border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:12px; overflow:hidden; background:#06090f; }
.tv-cell-head { display:flex; align-items:baseline; justify-content:space-between; padding:8px 11px; border-bottom:1px solid var(--line); background:rgba(4,8,14,.5); }
.tv-cell-head strong { font-family:var(--mono); font-size:.72rem; color:var(--green); }
.tv-cell-head span { color:var(--muted-2); font-size:.54rem; letter-spacing:.08em; text-transform:uppercase; }
.tv-slot { height: 240px; position:relative; }
```
On mobile 1 column, height 220.

`renderChart()`:
```js
const TIMEFRAMES = [
  { key: "1", label: "1m", role: "Trigger" },
  { key: "5", label: "5m", role: "Signal" },
  { key: "15", label: "15m", role: "Structure" },
  { key: "240", label: "4h", role: "Context" },
];

function renderChart() {
  const symbol = state.selectedSymbol;
  const tvSymbol = `BINANCE:${symbol}`;
  $("chart-symbol").textContent = symbol;
  $("chart-link").href = ...
  updateChartPrice();
  for (const tf of TIMEFRAMES) {
    renderTradingViewWidget($(`tv-${tf.key}`), tvSymbol, tf.key);
  }
  scheduleChartFallback();
}

function renderTradingViewWidget(host, tvSymbol, interval) {
  if (!host) return;
  host.innerHTML = '<div class="chart-loading">Loading…</div>';
  const container = document.createElement("div");
  container.className = "tradingview-widget-container";
  container.style.height = "100%";
  const widget = document.createElement("div");
  widget.className = "tradingview-widget-container__widget";
  widget.style.height = "100%";
  container.appendChild(widget);
  const script = document.createElement("script");
  script.type = "text/javascript";
  script.async = true;
  script.src = "https://s3.tradingview.com/external-embedding/embed-widget-advanced-chart.js";
  script.textContent = JSON.stringify({
    autosize: true,
    symbol: tvSymbol,
    interval,
    timezone: "Etc/UTC",
    theme: "dark",
